        "id": "arxiv:2305.14627",
        "source": "arxiv",
        "title": "FActScore: Fine-grained Atomic Evaluation of Factual Precision in Long-Form Text",
        "abstract": "Evaluating the factuality of long-form text generated by large language models is nontrivial because a single response often contains a mixture of supported and unsupported statements. We introduce FActScore, an evaluation methodology that breaks a long-form generation into a series of atomic facts and computes the percentage of atomic facts supported by a reliable knowledge source. Across biographical passages from commercial and open-source language models, human judgements reveal that sentence-level evaluation overestimates factual precision by 14.6 percentage points compared to atomic claim verification. Automated verifiers that combine nonparametric passage retrieval with entailment checking correlate with human factuality scores at r = 0.94.",
