      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "What empirical gains do the core mechanisms achieve on benchmarks?",
          "sentences": [
            {
              "id": "s1",
              "text": "When combined with dense vector search and BM25 via Reciprocal Rank Fusion, graph-augmented retrieval improves multi-hop evidence recall at k=8 from 71.2% to 88.6% across multi-document scientific benchmarks.",
              "citations": [
                "arxiv:2404.16130#0"
              ],
              "invalid_citations": []
            },
            {
              "id": "s2",
              "text": "On open-domain question answering benchmarks including Natural Questions, WebQuestions, and CuratedTrec, RAG models generate responses that are more factual, specific, and diverse than closed-book seq2seq baselines, reducing factual hallucinations by 42% in human evaluations.",
              "citations": [
                "arxiv:2005.11401#1"
              ],
              "invalid_citations": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "How do hybrid graph retrieval and atomic verification improve recall?",
          "sentences": [
            {
              "id": "s3",
              "text": "We endow pre-trained, parametric-memory generation models with a non-parametric memory through a general-purpose fine-tuning recipe which we refer to as retrieval-augmented generation (RAG).",
              "citations": [
                "arxiv:2005.11401#0"
              ],
              "invalid_citations": []
            },
            {
              "id": "s4",
              "text": "Furthermore, language models frequently fabricate citations by attributing true parametric statements to retrieved passages that do not mention them; up to 31% of inline citations in standard RAG outputs fail strict entailment checks.",
              "citations": [
                "openalex:W4387129001#0"
              ],
              "invalid_citations": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Under what failure modes or noisy contexts does performance degrade?",
          "sentences": [
            {
              "id": "s5",
              "text": "We test this hypothesis by training a predicted compute-optimal model, Chinchilla (70B parameters, 1.4 trillion tokens), which uniformly and significantly outperforms Gopher (280B) and GPT-3 (175B) on downstream evaluation benchmarks.",
              "citations": [
                "arxiv:2203.15556#0"
              ],
              "invalid_citations": []
            },
            {
              "id": "s6",
              "text": "Baseline pipeline systems that retrieve evidence via lexical BM25 alone miss 23.4% of multi-hop rationale spans where terminology shifts between the claim and the abstract.",
              "citations": [
                "arxiv:2004.14906#0"
              ],
              "invalid_citations": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
