    "report_markdown": "# Verified Synthesis Report (Revised after Skeptic Audit)\n\n> **Research question:** Do retrieval-augmented generation models reduce factual hallucinations compared to closed-book LLMs, and under what conditions do RAG citations fail?\n>\n> **Verification summary:** 6/6 claims supported (100%), 0 refuted, 0 insufficient evidence \u00b7 citation accuracy 100% \u00b7 5 sources cited (8 retrieved) \u00b7 2 skeptic audit round(s)\n\n## What empirical gains do the core mechanisms achieve on benchmarks?\n\n- **s1.** When combined with dense vector search and BM25 via Reciprocal Rank Fusion, graph-augmented retrieval improves multi-hop evidence recall at k=8 from 71.2% to 88.6% across multi-document scientific benchmarks. [1]\n  - `SUPPORTED (0.97)` _When combined with dense vector search and BM25 via Reciprocal Rank Fusion, graph-augmented retrieval improves multi-hop evidence recall at k=8 from 71.2% to 88.6% across multi-document scientific benchmarks._\n    - [1] (Edge et al., 2024, chars 396\u2013604, exact): \"When combined with dense vector search and BM25 via Reciprocal Rank Fusion, graph-augmented retrieval improves multi-hop evidence recall at k=8 from 71.2% to 88.6% across multi-document scientific benchmarks.\"\n- **s2.** On open-domain question answering benchmarks including Natural Questions, WebQuestions, and CuratedTrec, RAG models generate responses that are more factual, specific, and diverse than closed-book seq2seq baselines, reducing factual hallucinations by 42% in human evaluations. [2]\n  - `SUPPORTED (0.97)` _On open-domain question answering benchmarks including Natural Questions, WebQuestions, and CuratedTrec, RAG models generate responses that are more factual, specific, and diverse than closed-book seq2seq baselines, reducing factual hallucinations by 42% in human evaluations._\n    - [2] (Lewis et al., 2020, chars 552\u2013828, exact): \"On open-domain question answering benchmarks including Natural Questions, WebQuestions, and CuratedTrec, RAG models generate responses that are more factual, specific, and diverse than closed-book seq2seq baselines, reducing factual hallucinations by 42% in human evaluations.\"\n\n## How do hybrid graph retrieval and atomic verification improve recall?\n\n- **s3.** We endow pre-trained, parametric-memory generation models with a non-parametric memory through a general-purpose fine-tuning recipe which we refer to as retrieval-augmented generation (RAG). [2]\n  - `SUPPORTED (0.97)` _We endow pre-trained, parametric-memory generation models with a non-parametric memory through a general-purpose fine-tuning recipe which we refer to as retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)._\n    - [2] (Lewis et al., 2020, chars 361\u2013551, exact): \"We endow pre-trained, parametric-memory generation models with a non-parametric memory through a general-purpose fine-tuning recipe which we refer to as retrieval-augmented generation (RAG).\"\n- **s4.** Furthermore, language models frequently fabricate citations by attributing true parametric statements to retrieved passages that do not mention them; up to 31% of inline citations in standard RAG outputs fail strict entailment checks. [3]\n  - `SUPPORTED (0.97)` _Furthermore, language models frequently fabricate citations by attributing true parametric statements to retrieved passages that do not mention them; up to 31% of inline citations in standard RAG outputs fail strict entailment checks._\n    - [3] (Petrova et al., 2024, chars 399\u2013633, exact): \"Furthermore, language models frequently fabricate citations by attributing true parametric statements to retrieved passages that do not mention them; up to 31% of inline citations in standard RAG outputs fail strict entailment checks.\"\n\n## Under what failure modes or noisy contexts does performance degrade?\n\n- **s5.** We test this hypothesis by training a predicted compute-optimal model, Chinchilla (70B parameters, 1.4 trillion tokens), which uniformly and significantly outperforms Gopher (280B) and GPT-3 (175B) on downstream evaluation benchmarks. [4]\n  - `SUPPORTED (0.97)` _We test this hypothesis by training a predicted compute-optimal model, Chinchilla (70B parameters, 1.4 trillion tokens), which uniformly and significantly outperforms Gopher (280B) and GPT-3 (175B) on downstream evaluation benchmarks._\n    - [4] (Hoffmann et al., 2022, chars 455\u2013689, exact): \"We test this hypothesis by training a predicted compute-optimal model, Chinchilla (70B parameters, 1.4 trillion tokens), which uniformly and significantly outperforms Gopher (280B) and GPT-3 (175B) on downstream evaluation benchmarks.\"\n- **s6.** Baseline pipeline systems that retrieve evidence via lexical BM25 alone miss 23.4% of multi-hop rationale spans where terminology shifts between the claim and the abstract. [5]\n  - `SUPPORTED (0.97)` _Baseline pipeline systems that retrieve evidence via lexical BM25 alone miss 23.4% of multi-hop rationale spans where terminology shifts between the claim and the abstract._\n    - [5] (Wadden et al., 2020, chars 353\u2013525, exact): \"Baseline pipeline systems that retrieve evidence via lexical BM25 alone miss 23.4% of multi-hop rationale spans where terminology shifts between the claim and the abstract.\"\n\n## Adversarial Skeptic Audit Log\n\n- **Round 1 (Revision Triggered):** Identified 1 unsubstantiated or refuted claim(s); requesting targeted counter-evidence retrieval and draft revision.\n- **Round 2 (Finalized):** All extracted claims are supported with high confidence.\n\n## References\n\n1. Darren Edge, Ha Trinh, Newman Cheng, Christopherul Larson (2024). **From Local to Global: A Graph RAG Approach to Query-Focused Summarization**. *arXiv*. [arxiv:2404.16130](https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.16130)\n2. Patrick Lewis, Ethan Perez, Aleksandra Piktus, Douwe Kiela (2020). **Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks**. *NeurIPS*. [arxiv:2005.11401](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.11401)\n3.  Elena Petrova, Marcus Chen, Surya Narayanan (2024). **When Retrieval Fails: Noisy Contexts and Hallucination Amplification in RAG**. *TACL*. [openalex:W4387129001](https://doi.org/10.1162/tacl_a_00651)\n4. Jordan Hoffmann, Sebastian Borgeaud, Arthur Mensch, Laurent Sifre (2022). **Training Compute-Optimal Large Language Models**. *NeurIPS*. [arxiv:2203.15556](https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556)\n5. David Wadden, Shanchuan Lin, Kyle Lo, Lucy Lu Wang et al. (2020). **Fact or Fiction: Verifying Scientific Claims**. *EMNLP*. [arxiv:2004.14906](https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.14906)\n"
